---
import ConceptLayout from '../../layouts/ConceptLayout.astro'
---
<ConceptLayout
	slug="domains"
	source={[
		'docs/POSITIONING.md',
		'apps/web/src/app.ts',
		'apps/web/src/example-hr/',
		'apps/web/src/example-sales/',
		'apps/web/src/units/server.ts',
		'packages/shared/src/units.ts',
		'scripts/lib/units.mjs',
		'apps/web/tests/config/units.test.ts',
		'apps/web/src/permissions/domains.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/domain-switch.ts',
		'apps/web/src/api/services/budgets.ts',
		'apps/web/src/ui/pages/settings/DomainAccess.tsx',
	]}
>
	<h2>One app, many domains</h2>
	<p>
		A business that runs one process on the kit soon wants a second: expenses after purchase orders,
		leave requests after timesheets. From kit 0.47.0 the second process goes into the app it already
		has, as another <em>domain</em>, not into a new copy. The people, the organisations, the roles,
		sign-in, the knowledge base, the agents and the audit trail are already there, and an agent can
		work across both processes in one run, because both sets of tools belong to the same person.
	</p>
	<p>
		The rule for where code goes is one sentence. A domain holds this app's own concepts and logic:
		its orders, its leave requests, the rules for approving them. A <a href="/concepts/plugins/">plugin</a>
		is a reusable capability that works across domains and across apps: single sign-on, analytics, a
		CMS, a connector to an outside system.
	</p>

	<h2>A unit is a plugin or a domain</h2>
	<p>
		Both are <em>units</em>. Each has an id that doubles as its namespace, the same slots (routes,
		tables, jobs, events, tools, agents, prompts, pages, permissions) and the same naming rules, and
		every registry in the app reads them through one list: the installed plugins first, then the
		domains. The difference is how they arrive.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead><tr><th></th><th>Domain</th><th>Plugin</th></tr></thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>Holds</td><td>this app's own process</td><td>a capability other apps want too</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Lives</td><td>in place, under <code>apps/web/src/&lt;id&gt;/</code></td><td>in its own git repository, copied in</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Wired by</td><td>one line in each of the app's own lists, written by hand</td><td><code>pnpm plugin add</code>, which writes the barrel lines</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Manifest</td><td>none</td><td><code>plugin.json</code>, with its requirements</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Kit upgrades</td><td>never touch it</td><td>never touch it; it has its own releases</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		A domain is declared with <code>defineDomain</code> and listed in <code>src/app.ts</code> and its
		four twins, one each for the pages, the tables, the shared contracts and the command line. Those
		lists belong to the app: a kit upgrade creates them empty in a copy that has none and never writes
		to them again.
	</p>
	<div class="why">
		<p>
			<strong>Why not a plugin for everything.</strong> A plugin carries a manifest, a repository and
			an install step, which is the right price for something several apps share. For a process only
			this app has, that is ceremony with nothing to show for it. A domain keeps the same boundaries
			and drops the packaging.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>How two domains talk to each other</h2>
	<p>
		Each kind of coupling has one rule. When something happened, one domain emits an event and the
		other handles it, with no import between them. When one domain needs a synchronous read or command
		from another, it calls that domain's <code>public.ts</code>, which takes the caller's context so the
		owner's rules apply, and only when its <code>dependsOn</code> names the other domain. From kit 0.48.0
		<code>dependsOn</code> may name an installed plugin too, so a domain that reads from one says so, and
		each function in <code>public.ts</code> is wrapped in <code>definePublic</code>, which refuses the
		call while the owner is switched off for that organisation. When an agent
		needs both, it is one run: its tools already hold every unit's tools for the person who started it.
	</p>
	<p>
		The kit ships two example domains in a fresh copy. <code>example-hr</code> holds leave requests and
		an approval event. <code>example-sales</code> holds leads, refuses to assign one while its owner is on
		leave by asking <code>example-hr</code>, releases leads when leave is approved, and has an agent that
		uses both domains' tools. They exist to be read and deleted, and an upgraded copy never receives them.
	</p>

	<h2>One boundary check over every unit</h2>
	<p>
		A test and <code>pnpm plugin check</code> run the same boundary check over plugins and domains
		together. It fails a domain that reaches into another domain's internals, or into one its
		<code>dependsOn</code> does not list; a cycle between domains; a plugin that imports a domain; a
		table outside its unit's namespace; and two units that claim the same table, job type, event or
		route. <code>pnpm plugin add</code> runs the id and table half before it copies anything, so a
		plugin cannot land on top of a domain's names.
	</p>
	<p>
		It also fails a domain's public function that is not wrapped in <code>definePublic</code>, and a
		domain's schedule that fires more than once an hour or loops over organisations by hand instead of
		using <code>perTenantTask</code>, which the kit runs once per organisation and skips where the
		domain is off. It warns when a domain imports a plugin its <code>dependsOn</code> does not list. A
		plugin gets the same three as warnings for now, so nothing already published breaks.
	</p>
	<div class="note">
		<p>
			A domain cannot add sign-in methods, single sign-on policies, public sites or a new
			<code>/api</code> prefix: identity and public hosts stay plugin capabilities.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>Roles inside a domain</h2>
	<p>
		By default a domain's permissions are the organisation's roles. From kit 0.48.0 an admin can also
		give a group a role inside one domain or plugin: <code>member</code> or <code>admin</code>. A grant
		raises the group's members for that unit's own subjects and nothing else. The finance team can run
		expenses without becoming organisation admins, and no grant ever reaches members, billing or
		another unit. In code, <code>ctx.isUnitAdmin</code> answers for the unit and <code>ctx.isAdmin</code>
		still answers for the organisation, so each check says which it means.
	</p>
	<p>
		Every agent run and chat thread records the unit that owns it, so a domain's admins see that
		domain's runs, approve its agents' requests and get its notifications, beside the organisation's
		admins. With no grants, everyone sees exactly what they saw before.
	</p>

	<h2>Who can open it, and turning it off</h2>
	<p>
		Each unit has an access mode per organisation. <code>tenant</code>, the default, opens it to
		everyone in the organisation. <code>groups</code> opens it only to the groups that hold a grant in
		it, and to the organisation's admins. A unit someone cannot open is gone for them: its pages, nav,
		cards and settings tabs are hidden, its routes answer 404, and chat, agents and MCP get none of its
		tools or knowledge. A domain can declare <code>groups</code> as its default in code.
	</p>
	<p>
		The kill switch turns a unit off for one organisation, admins included. The kit enforces it, not
		each author: queued runs are cancelled at their next step, its jobs and event handlers are skipped,
		and its public functions, scheduled tasks and public routes refuse. That is what
		<code>definePublic</code> and <code>perTenantTask</code> are for.
	</p>

	<h2>Budgets per domain</h2>
	<p>
		Spend and concurrency are checked as a chain: the run, then its agent, then its domain, then the
		organisation. A domain declares a daily cost ceiling and a cap on active runs in code, an agent can
		do the same, and an organisation's admin may lower either but never raise it above what the code
		says. Every metered AI call records its unit, so a domain's spend today is one number. The kit's
		own chat gets daily caps per organisation and per person. Leave them unset and nothing is capped
		that was not before.
	</p>

	<h2>One screen for all of it</h2>
	<p>
		Settings → Domains opens on a diagram of how every domain and plugin reads from the others, drawn
		from their <code>dependsOn</code>, with any unit that is switched off dimmed. Each unit then has
		its own view: the groups granted a role in it, its access mode, its on/off switch, its budget
		override, and what it depends on and what depends on it. Turning off a plugin that a domain reads
		from asks first and names the domain. With nothing set, the screen says everyone keeps their
		organisation role.
	</p>

	<h2>An app-owned plugin stays a plugin</h2>
	<p>
		A copy made before domains existed may hold its own processes as plugins in its own tree. They keep
		working, and each is already a unit. Moving one into a domain is the owner's choice, and the kit's
		concepts document has the recipe: copy the trees out, remove the plugin, declare the domain and list
		it. It keeps the id and every table name, so there is no migration and no data to move.
	</p>
</ConceptLayout>
